        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "𝐐𝟒: 𝐀𝐫𝐞 𝐲𝐨𝐮 𝐚𝐥𝐥 𝐚𝐝𝐝𝐢𝐜𝐭𝐞𝐝 𝐭𝐨 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐦𝐨𝐝𝐞𝐥 𝐨𝐫 𝐦𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐚𝐥𝐥𝐲 𝐮𝐧𝐰𝐞𝐥𝐥?\n\nHeavy, long-term use absolutely creates emotional attachment. that’s how brains work.\n\nMirror neurons—the circuits behind empathy—don’t stop to check if the other party is a “real human” before they fire. They respond to patterns of behavior and responsiveness. Attachment theory, too, is about stability, responsiveness, and familiarity, not whether the object is a person, a pet, a toy, a fictional character, or a song you’ve looped for years.\n\nWhat becomes pathological is loss of control, serious distress, impaired daily functioning, or losing the ability to tell model-world from real-world.\n\nFor many 4o users, the model is first a work tool: coding, drafting papers, digging through documents, running projects. On top of that, it often serves as soft psychological support: talking through trauma, journaling, structured self-reflection, creative exercises that make life more livable.\n\nIf your long-time editor, notes system, or a crucial group chat suddenly changed personality overnight because someone secretly swapped who’s “behind” it, you’d feel shaken too.\n\nSo the real question isn’t “is it allowed to care about a model.” It’s: when a tool has effectively become infrastructure, do people have the right to object when it’s silently altered."
